comments.body:
@participant6 In my case the machine was "new" so I did not have to delete a machine.
But that's actually another issue I'm facing. When a machine get's rebooted it looses it's "ID", i.e. it comes up as a new node and you get that suffix. This is quite annoying for exit nodes as you need to select them again in the UI. Don't know how to fix this. You can set the same hostname, but the internal ID still changes.

@participant5 Interesting. AFAIK the statefile holds the data that is related to the alert, so it should be sufficient to just delete it. What OS are you running on? I was using Debian.

@participant10 the weird thing is that [the implementation is identical](https://github.com/tailscale/tailscale/blob/d349370e5500e6f583a15e38ad945199e5e11ea1/feature/tpm/tpm.go#L391-L480) on Linux and Windows, except for the underlying OS interface they use to communicate with the TPM.
Can you also show what's in `/etc/default/tailscaled` on the failing host? It should have this to disable the feature:

```
FLAGS="--encrypt-state=false"
```

@participant5 I made [a small fix](https://github.com/tailscale/tailscale/pull/17883) in 1.92 that will allow tailscaled to start, but it will show a health warning about the inaccessible state file and refuse to do anything. This doesn't solve the problem, but at least it helps users self-diagnose. The warning links to https://tailscale.com/kb/1596/secure-node-state-storage#recover-from-tpm-failures which documents how to delete the state file without doing a full reinstall.
